ML_PAGE("formulas", "<header class=\"page-header theme-exam\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">27 FAMILIES · 101 FORMULAS · RANKED</span><h2>Formula Sheet</h2><p>Every formula in Chapters 1 to 5, explicit and implicit, ranked by how likely it is to matter in a calculation test. Each card opens with the <b>base formula</b>; its rearrangements and special cases sit underneath at lower priority. Use Recall mode to hide the base formulas and test yourself.</p></header><div class=\"ff-controls\"><div class=\"ff-chips\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Filter by chapter\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip on\" data-ff-chip=\"all\">All chapters</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c1\">Chapter 1</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c2\">Chapter 2</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c3\">Chapter 3</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c4a\">Appendix 4A (TVM)</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c4\">Chapter 4</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c5\">Chapter 5</button></div><div class=\"ff-tools\"><input id=\"ff-search\" class=\"ff-search\" type=\"search\" placeholder=\"Search: beta, margin, annuity, spread...\" aria-label=\"Search formulas\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn ff-recall\" data-ff-recall aria-pressed=\"false\">Recall mode</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-action=\"print\">Print</button></div></div><p class=\"ff-empty\" hidden>No formulas match. Clear the search or pick another chapter.</p><details class=\"ff-index\"><summary>One-line index of all 27 families</summary><div class=\"scroll-x\"><table class=\"comparison-table ff-table\"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Family</th><th>Base formula</th><th>Chapter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4a\"><td>1</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-1\">Time value of a lump sum</button></td><td>$$FV = PV\\,(1+r)^n$$</td><td>Appendix 4A</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c5\"><td>2</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-2\">CAPM and the Security Market Line</button></td><td>$$R_i = R_f + \\beta_i\\,\\bigl[E(R_m) - R_f\\bigr]$$</td><td>Chapter 5</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c5\"><td>3</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-3\">Two-asset portfolio risk</button></td><td>$$\\sigma_p = \\sqrt{w_A^2\\sigma_A^2 + w_B^2\\sigma_B^2 + 2\\,w_A w_B\\,\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\,\\rho_{AB}}$$</td><td>Chapter 5</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>4</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-4\">Margin</button></td><td>$$\\text{Margin} = \\dfrac{V - D}{V} = \\dfrac{\\text{investor equity}}{\\text{market value}}$$</td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c5\"><td>5</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-5\">Portfolio beta</button></td><td>$$\\beta_p = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\\,\\beta_i$$</td><td>Chapter 5</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c5\"><td>6</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-6\">Portfolio expected return</button></td><td>$$E(R_p) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\\,E(R_i),\\qquad \\sum w_i = 1$$</td><td>Chapter 5</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4\"><td>7</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-7\">Holding period return (HPR)</button></td><td>$$HPR = \\dfrac{\\text{income} + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0}$$</td><td>Chapter 4</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4\"><td>8</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-8\">Required return</button></td><td>$$R_{\\text{required}} = R_{\\text{real}} + IP + RP = R_f + RP$$</td><td>Chapter 4</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>9</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-9\">Return on equity with margin</button></td><td>$$\\text{Return} = \\dfrac{\\text{income} - \\text{interest} + V_1 - V_0}{E_0}$$</td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4a\"><td>10</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-10\">Ordinary annuity</button></td><td>$$FV = PMT\\left[\\dfrac{(1+r)^n - 1}{r}\\right]$$</td><td>Appendix 4A</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4a\"><td>11</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-11\">Value of a cash-flow stream</button></td><td>$$PV = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\dfrac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}$$</td><td>Appendix 4A</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4\"><td>12</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-12\">Yield, IRR and growth rate</button></td><td>$$r = \\left(\\dfrac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n} - 1$$</td><td>Chapter 4</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>13</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-13\">Short sale</button></td><td>$$\\text{Profit} = (P_{\\text{sell}} - P_{\\text{buy back}}) \\times N$$</td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4\"><td>14</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-14\">Standard deviation and CV</button></td><td>$$s = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sum_{t=1}^{n} (R_t - \\bar{R})^2}{n - 1}}$$</td><td>Chapter 4</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c1 c4\"><td>15</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-15\">Real return</button></td><td>$$R_{\\text{real}} \\approx R_{\\text{nominal}} - i$$</td><td>Chapter 1, Chapter 4</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c1\"><td>16</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-16\">After-tax return</button></td><td>$$R_{\\text{after-tax}} = R_{\\text{pre-tax}}\\,(1 - t)$$</td><td>Chapter 1</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>17</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-17\">Bid, ask and spread</button></td><td>$$\\text{Spread} = \\text{Ask} - \\text{Bid},\\qquad \\text{Bid} &lt; \\text{Ask}$$</td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>18</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-18\">Home-currency return</button></td><td>$$(1 + R_{\\text{home}}) = (1 + R_{\\text{asset}})\\,(1 + R_{FX})$$</td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c3\"><td>19</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-19\">Net trading profit and costs</button></td><td>$$\\text{Net profit} = N\\,(P_1 - P_0) - (C_{\\text{buy}} + C_{\\text{sell}})$$</td><td>Chapter 3</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c3\"><td>20</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-20\">Order execution rules</button></td><td>$$\\text{Buy limit fills if } P \\le L,\\qquad \\text{Sell limit fills if } P \\ge L$$</td><td>Chapter 3</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c3\"><td>21</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-21\">Relative performance</button></td><td>$$\\text{Relative performance} = R_{\\text{portfolio}} - R_{\\text{benchmark}}$$</td><td>Chapter 3</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>22</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-22\">Long purchase</button></td><td>$$\\text{Profit} = N\\,(P_1 - P_0) + \\text{dividends},\\qquad R = \\dfrac{P_1 - P_0}{P_0}$$</td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c3\"><td>23</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-23\">Index construction</button></td><td>$$\\text{Price-weighted change} = \\dfrac{\\sum P_1}{\\sum P_0} - 1$$</td><td>Chapter 3</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>24</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-24\">Underwriting spread</button></td><td>$$\\text{Spread} = P_{\\text{offer}} - P_{\\text{paid to issuer}}$$</td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c1 c3\"><td>25</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-25\">Bond prices, rates and yield</button></td><td>$$r \\uparrow \\;\\Rightarrow\\; P_{\\text{existing bond}} \\downarrow$$</td><td>Chapter 1, Chapter 3</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c1\"><td>26</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-26\">Liquidity discount</button></td><td>$$\\text{Discount} = \\dfrac{\\text{fair value} - \\text{quick-sale price}}{\\text{fair value}}$$</td><td>Chapter 1</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4 c5\"><td>27</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-27\">Risk and return identities</button></td><td>$$\\text{Total risk} = \\text{systematic (market)} + \\text{unsystematic (firm-specific)}$$</td><td>Chapter 4, Chapter 5</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><div class=\"ff-list\"><div class=\"ff-tier\" data-ff-tier=\"1\"><h3 class=\"ff-tier-title\"><span>Tier 1</span> Must know</h3><p class=\"ff-tier-note\">The formulas that carry most of the marks in a calculation test. Learn the base formula cold, then its variants.</p><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-1\" data-ff-ch=\"c4a\" data-ff-text=\"time value of a lump sum  pv  = amount today,  r  = rate per period,  n  = number of periods. move one amount forward in time (compounding). never compare cash at different dates directly. the exponent counts compounding periods.   text rm 1 , 300  is the simple-interest answer; the extra   text rm 31  is interest on interest. present value (discounting) compounded  m  times a year effective annual rate simple interest continuous compounding doubling time (rule of 72)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">1</span><h3>Time value of a lump sum</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4a\">Appendix 4A</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$FV = PV\\,(1+r)^n$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$PV$ = amount today, $r$ = rate per period, $n$ = number of periods.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Move one amount forward in time (compounding). Never compare cash at different dates directly.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$FV = \\text{RM}1{,}000\\,(1.10)^3 = \\text{RM}1{,}331$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The exponent counts compounding periods. $\\text{RM}1{,}300$ is the simple-interest answer; the extra $\\text{RM}31$ is interest on interest.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Present value (discounting)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PV = \\dfrac{FV}{(1+r)^n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Compounded $m$ times a year</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$FV = PV\\left(1+\\dfrac{r}{m}\\right)^{mt}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Effective annual rate</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EAR = \\left(1+\\dfrac{r}{m}\\right)^{m} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Simple interest</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$FV = PV\\,(1 + rn)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Continuous compounding</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EAR = e^{r} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Doubling time (rule of 72)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$n = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{\\ln(1+r)} \\approx \\dfrac{72}{r\\%}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-tvm\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-2\" data-ff-ch=\"c5\" data-ff-text=\"capm and the security market line  r_i  = required return,  r_f  = risk-free rate,   beta_i  = beta,  e(r_m)  = expected market return. find the return an investor should demand for a given beta; compare it with the forecast. beta multiplies the premium, not the market return.  5 % + 1.2  times 11 % = 18.2 %  is wrong; the right answer is  5 % + 1.2 ,(11 % - 5 %) = 12.2 % . market risk premium alpha (above the sml = undervalued) sml as a straight line solve for beta\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">2</span><h3>CAPM and the Security Market Line</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$R_i = R_f + \\beta_i\\,\\bigl[E(R_m) - R_f\\bigr]$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$R_i$ = required return, $R_f$ = risk-free rate, $\\beta_i$ = beta, $E(R_m)$ = expected market return.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Find the return an investor should demand for a given beta; compare it with the forecast.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$4\\% + 1.5\\,(6\\%) = 13\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Beta multiplies the premium, not the market return. $5\\% + 1.2 \\times 11\\% = 18.2\\%$ is wrong; the right answer is $5\\% + 1.2\\,(11\\% - 5\\%) = 12.2\\%$.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Market risk premium</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$MRP = E(R_m) - R_f$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Alpha (above the SML = undervalued)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\alpha_i = E(R_i) - R_i^{\\text{CAPM}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">SML as a straight line</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_i = R_f + (\\text{slope})\\,\\beta_i,\\quad \\text{slope} = E(R_m) - R_f$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Solve for beta</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\beta_i = \\dfrac{R_i - R_f}{E(R_m) - R_f}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-capm\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-3\" data-ff-ch=\"c5\" data-ff-text=\"two-asset portfolio risk  w  = weights (sum to 1),   sigma  = standard deviations,   rho_ ab   = correlation. risk of a mix of two assets. the lower the correlation, the lower   sigma_p . portfolio risk is not the weighted average of the risks (that is only true when the correlation is  +1 ). zero correlation does not mean zero risk. perfect positive correlation,   rho = +1  perfect negative correlation,   rho = -1  weights that make risk zero (  rho = -1 ) covariance correlation, always between  -1  and  +1  minimum-variance weight (extension)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">3</span><h3>Two-asset portfolio risk</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\sigma_p = \\sqrt{w_A^2\\sigma_A^2 + w_B^2\\sigma_B^2 + 2\\,w_A w_B\\,\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\,\\rho_{AB}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$w$ = weights (sum to 1), $\\sigma$ = standard deviations, $\\rho_{AB}$ = correlation.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Risk of a mix of two assets. The lower the correlation, the lower $\\sigma_p$.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} w = 0.5,\\ \\sigma_A = 20\\%,\\ \\sigma_B = 10\\%,\\ \\rho = 0 \\\\ \\sigma_p = \\sqrt{0.25(0.04) + 0.25(0.01)} = \\sqrt{0.0125} \\approx 11.18\\% \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Portfolio risk is NOT the weighted average of the risks (that is only true when the correlation is $+1$). Zero correlation does not mean zero risk.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Perfect positive correlation, $\\rho = +1$</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\sigma_p = w_A\\sigma_A + w_B\\sigma_B$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Perfect negative correlation, $\\rho = -1$</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\sigma_p = \\bigl|\\,w_A\\sigma_A - w_B\\sigma_B\\,\\bigr|$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Weights that make risk zero ($\\rho = -1$)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$w_A\\sigma_A = w_B\\sigma_B \\;\\Rightarrow\\; w_A = \\dfrac{\\sigma_B}{\\sigma_A + \\sigma_B}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Covariance</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\operatorname{Cov}(R_A, R_B) = \\rho_{AB}\\,\\sigma_A\\sigma_B$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Correlation, always between $-1$ and $+1$</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\rho_{AB} = \\dfrac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_A, R_B)}{\\sigma_A\\sigma_B}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Minimum-variance weight (extension)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$w_A^{*} = \\dfrac{\\sigma_B^2 - \\rho\\sigma_A\\sigma_B}{\\sigma_A^2 + \\sigma_B^2 - 2\\rho\\sigma_A\\sigma_B}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-portfolio\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-4\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"margin  v  = current market value of the securities,  d  = loan (debit balance). check whether a leveraged position is still safe. a margin call comes when margin falls below the maintenance level. margin is not the amount borrowed. the loan does not shrink when the price falls, so margin falls faster than the price. initial equity and loan margin-call value margin-call price per share deposit that restores the margin\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">4</span><h3>Margin</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Margin} = \\dfrac{V - D}{V} = \\dfrac{\\text{investor equity}}{\\text{market value}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$V$ = current market value of the securities, $D$ = loan (debit balance).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Check whether a leveraged position is still safe. A margin call comes when margin falls below the maintenance level.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$V = 10{,}000,\\ D = 4{,}000 \\to 60\\%;\\quad V \\to 7{,}000:\\ \\dfrac{3{,}000}{7{,}000} = 42.86\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Margin is not the amount borrowed. The loan does not shrink when the price falls, so margin falls faster than the price.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Initial equity and loan</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$E_0 = V_0\\, m_{\\text{initial}},\\qquad D = V_0\\,(1 - m_{\\text{initial}})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Margin-call value</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$V^{*} = \\dfrac{D}{1 - m}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Margin-call price per share</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P^{*} = \\dfrac{D}{N\\,(1 - m)}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Deposit that restores the margin</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Deposit} = D - V\\,(1 - m)$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-margin\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-5\" data-ff-ch=\"c5\" data-ff-text=\"portfolio beta  w_i  = weight of asset  i ,   beta_i  = its beta. market sensitivity of a portfolio. unlike   sigma_p , it is a plain weighted average. do not take an unweighted average. low beta does not mean low total risk (  beta  neq  sigma ). move of the market beta from data (extension) market beta weight needed for a target beta\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">5</span><h3>Portfolio beta</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\beta_p = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\\,\\beta_i$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$w_i$ = weight of asset $i$, $\\beta_i$ = its beta.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Market sensitivity of a portfolio. Unlike $\\sigma_p$, it is a plain weighted average.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$0.4(1.5) + 0.6(0.5) = 0.90$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Do not take an unweighted average. Low beta does not mean low total risk ($\\beta \\neq \\sigma$).</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Move of the market</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\Delta R_i \\approx \\beta_i\\,\\Delta R_m$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Beta from data (extension)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\beta_i = \\dfrac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_i, R_m)}{\\operatorname{Var}(R_m)} = \\rho_{i,m}\\,\\dfrac{\\sigma_i}{\\sigma_m}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Market beta</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\beta_m = 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Weight needed for a target beta</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$w_A = \\dfrac{\\beta^{*} - \\beta_B}{\\beta_A - \\beta_B}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-beta\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-6\" data-ff-ch=\"c5\" data-ff-text=\"portfolio expected return  w_i  = weight,  e(r_i)  = expected return of asset  i . return of any mix of assets: always a weighted average. use the weights, not the simple mean of the returns. weight needed for a target return return in one period\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">6</span><h3>Portfolio expected return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$E(R_p) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\\,E(R_i),\\qquad \\sum w_i = 1$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$w_i$ = weight, $E(R_i)$ = expected return of asset $i$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Return of any mix of assets: always a weighted average.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$0.50(12\\%) + 0.30(8\\%) + 0.20(5\\%) = 9.4\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use the weights, not the simple mean of the returns.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Weight needed for a target return</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$w_A = \\dfrac{R^{*} - R_B}{R_A - R_B}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return in one period</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{p,t} = \\sum_i w_i R_{i,t}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-portfolio\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-7\" data-ff-ch=\"c4\" data-ff-text=\"holding period return (hpr)  p_0  = price paid,  p_1  = price at the end, income = dividends or interest received. return over one holding period, as a percentage of what you paid. hpr ignores time:  40 %  over  5  years is not comparable with  30 %  in  1  year. price change alone is not total return. total return in ringgit two parts of hpr capital gain (paper gain until sold) percentage return\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">7</span><h3>Holding period return (HPR)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$HPR = \\dfrac{\\text{income} + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$P_0$ = price paid, $P_1$ = price at the end, income = dividends or interest received.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Return over one holding period, as a percentage of what you paid.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{5 + 8}{100} = 13\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>HPR ignores time: $40\\%$ over $5$ years is not comparable with $30\\%$ in $1$ year. Price change alone is not total return.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Total return in ringgit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Total return} = \\text{income} + (P_1 - P_0)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Two parts of HPR</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{\\text{income}}{P_0} + \\dfrac{P_1 - P_0}{P_0}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Capital gain (paper gain until sold)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Capital gain} = P_1 - P_0$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Percentage return</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Return} = \\dfrac{\\text{profit}}{\\text{amount invested}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-hpr\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-8\" data-ff-ch=\"c4\" data-ff-text=\"required return  ip  = inflation premium,  rp  = risk premium,  r_f  = risk-free rate. the hurdle an investment must clear to be worth its risk. do not leave out the inflation premium, and do not confuse the risk premium with the total return. risk-free rate hurdle test\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">8</span><h3>Required return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$R_{\\text{required}} = R_{\\text{real}} + IP + RP = R_f + RP$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$IP$ = inflation premium, $RP$ = risk premium, $R_f$ = risk-free rate.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The hurdle an investment must clear to be worth its risk.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$2\\% + 3\\% + 4\\% = 9\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Do not leave out the inflation premium, and do not confuse the risk premium with the total return.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Risk-free rate</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_f = R_{\\text{real}} + IP$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Hurdle test</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$E(R) - R_{\\text{required}} \\ge 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{acceptable}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-return\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-9\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"return on equity with margin  v_0, v_1  = value at purchase and at sale,  e_0  = your own money at the start. a margin trade measured against your own equity, with interest and income. include both income and interest, and divide by initial equity, not by the whole position. without costs change in equity leverage scales volatility\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">9</span><h3>Return on equity with margin</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Return} = \\dfrac{\\text{income} - \\text{interest} + V_1 - V_0}{E_0}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$V_0, V_1$ = value at purchase and at sale, $E_0$ = your own money at the start.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A margin trade measured against your own equity, with interest and income.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{100 - 125 + 7{,}500 - 5{,}000}{2{,}500} = 99\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Include both income and interest, and divide by INITIAL equity, not by the whole position.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Without costs</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_E = \\dfrac{\\Delta V}{E_0} = \\dfrac{R_{\\text{asset}}}{m_{\\text{initial}}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Change in equity</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{E_1 - E_0}{E_0}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Leverage scales volatility</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\sigma_E \\approx \\dfrac{\\sigma_{\\text{asset}}}{m_{\\text{initial}}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-margin\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-10\" data-ff-ch=\"c4a\" data-ff-text=\"ordinary annuity  pmt  = equal payment at the end of each period. equal payments at equal intervals: savings plans, sinking funds. the last payment earns no interest. a payment at time 0 makes it an annuity due. present value of an annuity annuity due (payments at the start) payment needed to reach a target loan instalment interest earned\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">10</span><h3>Ordinary annuity</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4a\">Appendix 4A</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$FV = PMT\\left[\\dfrac{(1+r)^n - 1}{r}\\right]$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$PMT$ = equal payment at the END of each period.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Equal payments at equal intervals: savings plans, sinking funds.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$1{,}000\\left[\\dfrac{1.08^8 - 1}{0.08}\\right] = \\text{RM}10{,}636.63$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The last payment earns no interest. A payment at time 0 makes it an annuity due.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Present value of an annuity</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PV = PMT\\left[\\dfrac{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}{r}\\right]$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Annuity due (payments at the start)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$FV_{\\text{due}} = FV_{\\text{ordinary}} \\times (1 + r)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Payment needed to reach a target</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PMT = \\dfrac{FV}{\\bigl[(1+r)^n - 1\\bigr]/r}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Loan instalment</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PMT = \\dfrac{PV}{\\bigl[1 - (1+r)^{-n}\\bigr]/r}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Interest earned</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Interest} = FV - n \\times PMT$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-annuity\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-11\" data-ff-ch=\"c4a\" data-ff-text=\"value of a cash-flow stream  cf_t  = cash flow at the end of period  t . unequal cash flows (mixed stream): discount each one separately. the annuity shortcut needs equal payments. never add up cash flows without discounting them. net present value satisfactory investment\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">11</span><h3>Value of a cash-flow stream</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4a\">Appendix 4A</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$PV = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\dfrac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$CF_t$ = cash flow at the end of period $t$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Unequal cash flows (mixed stream): discount each one separately.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} CF = 30,\\ 40,\\ 50,\\ 60,\\ 70 \\ \\text{at } 8\\% \\\\ PV = \\dfrac{30}{1.08} + \\dfrac{40}{1.08^2} + \\dfrac{50}{1.08^3} + \\dfrac{60}{1.08^4} + \\dfrac{70}{1.08^5} = 193.51 \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The annuity shortcut needs EQUAL payments. Never add up cash flows without discounting them.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net present value</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NPV = PV(\\text{benefits}) - PV(\\text{costs})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Satisfactory investment</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PV(\\text{benefits}) \\ge PV(\\text{costs})$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-annuity\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-12\" data-ff-ch=\"c4\" data-ff-text=\"yield, irr and growth rate the compound annual rate that turns  pv  into  fv  in  n  years. annualise a multi-year return so different horizons can be compared.  40 %  over  5  years is not  40 % / 5 = 8 %  a year. irr of a stream of cash flows irr decision rule growth rate geometric average return\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">12</span><h3>Yield, IRR and growth rate</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$r = \\left(\\dfrac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n} - 1$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">The compound annual rate that turns $PV$ into $FV$ in $n$ years.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Annualise a multi-year return so different horizons can be compared.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\left(\\dfrac{1{,}400}{1{,}000}\\right)^{1/5} - 1 \\approx 6.96\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>$40\\%$ over $5$ years is NOT $40\\% / 5 = 8\\%$ a year.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">IRR of a stream of cash flows</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\dfrac{CF_t}{(1 + IRR)^t}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">IRR decision rule</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$IRR \\ge R_{\\text{required}} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{acceptable}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Growth rate</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$g = \\left(\\dfrac{D_n}{D_0}\\right)^{1/n} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Geometric average return</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\bar{R}_{\\text{geo}} = \\Bigl[\\prod_{t=1}^{n} (1 + R_t)\\Bigr]^{1/n} - 1$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-hpr\">Practise in the lesson</button></article></div><div class=\"ff-tier\" data-ff-tier=\"2\"><h3 class=\"ff-tier-title\"><span>Tier 2</span> Should know</h3><p class=\"ff-tier-note\">Common in calculation questions or as the second step of a longer one.</p><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-13\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"short sale  n  = number of shares borrowed and sold. borrow, sell, buy back, return. you win if the price falls. a rising price hurts the short seller, and the loss has no ceiling. the best case is the sale proceeds. payoff limits funds held by the broker short-account margin margin-call price\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">13</span><h3>Short sale</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Profit} = (P_{\\text{sell}} - P_{\\text{buy back}}) \\times N$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$N$ = number of shares borrowed and sold.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Borrow, sell, buy back, return. You win if the price falls.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$(25 - 31) \\times 300 = -\\text{RM}1{,}800$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A rising price hurts the short seller, and the loss has no ceiling. The best case is the sale proceeds.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Payoff limits</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Max gain} = N \\times P_{\\text{sell}},\\qquad \\text{Max loss} = \\text{unlimited}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Funds held by the broker</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Funds held} = \\text{proceeds} + \\text{margin deposit}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Short-account margin</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Margin} = \\dfrac{\\text{funds held} - N P}{N P}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Margin-call price</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P^{*} = \\dfrac{\\text{funds held}}{N\\,(1 + m)}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-short\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-14\" data-ff-ch=\"c4\" data-ff-text=\"standard deviation and cv  r_t  = return in period  t ,   bar r  = average return,  n - 1  for a sample. absolute spread of returns around the mean. use  n - 1  when told it is a sample. a historical   sigma  is an estimate, not a promise. average return coefficient of variation (risk per unit of return)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">14</span><h3>Standard deviation and CV</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$s = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sum_{t=1}^{n} (R_t - \\bar{R})^2}{n - 1}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$R_t$ = return in period $t$, $\\bar R$ = average return, $n - 1$ for a sample.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Absolute spread of returns around the mean.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$3, 6, 9, 12, 15:\\ \\ s = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{90}{4}} \\approx 4.74\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use $n - 1$ when told it is a sample. A historical $\\sigma$ is an estimate, not a promise.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Average return</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\bar{R} = \\dfrac{\\sum R_t}{n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Coefficient of variation (risk per unit of return)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$CV = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\bar{r}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-risk\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-15\" data-ff-ch=\"c1 c4\" data-ff-text=\"real return  i  = inflation rate. this is the quick approximation. what your money really earns after prices rise. use the approximation when it is asked for; the exact (fisher) answer is slightly different. exact (fisher equation) purchasing power of a nominal amount after tax and inflation\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">15</span><h3>Real return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c1\">Chapter 1</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$R_{\\text{real}} \\approx R_{\\text{nominal}} - i$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$i$ = inflation rate. This is the quick approximation.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> What your money really earns after prices rise.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$8\\% - 5\\% \\approx 3\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use the approximation when it is asked for; the exact (Fisher) answer is slightly different.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Exact (Fisher equation)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{real}} = \\dfrac{1 + R_{\\text{nominal}}}{1 + i} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Purchasing power of a nominal amount</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Real value} = \\dfrac{\\text{nominal value}}{1 + i}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">After tax and inflation</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{real}} \\approx R(1 - t) - i$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-return\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-16\" data-ff-ch=\"c1\" data-ff-text=\"after-tax return  t  = tax rate. compare investments on what you actually keep. a higher pre-tax return is not automatically better. apply the tax first.\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">16</span><h3>After-tax return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c1\">Chapter 1</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$R_{\\text{after-tax}} = R_{\\text{pre-tax}}\\,(1 - t)$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$t$ = tax rate.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Compare investments on what you actually keep.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$10\\%(1 - 0.30) = 7.0\\%\\quad\\text{vs}\\quad 8\\%(1 - 0.05) = 7.6\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A higher pre-tax return is not automatically better. Apply the tax first.</p></div></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l1-life\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-17\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"bid, ask and spread quotes are from the dealer&#x27;s side: the dealer buys at the bid and sells at the ask. what a dealer trade costs you. if you sell, you receive the bid; if you buy, you pay the ask. &quot;ask&quot; is not what the investor asks. selling and buying round-trip cost percentage trading cost\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">17</span><h3>Bid, ask and spread</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Spread} = \\text{Ask} - \\text{Bid},\\qquad \\text{Bid} &lt; \\text{Ask}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Quotes are from the dealer's side: the dealer buys at the bid and sells at the ask.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> What a dealer trade costs you.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$100.00 - 99.50 = 0.50$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>If you sell, you receive the bid; if you buy, you pay the ask. \"Ask\" is not what the investor asks.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Selling and buying</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Proceeds} = N \\times \\text{Bid},\\qquad \\text{Cost} = N \\times \\text{Ask}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Round-trip cost</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$N\\,(\\text{Ask} - \\text{Bid})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Percentage trading cost</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Cost} = c \\times \\text{trade value}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-trading\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-18\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"home-currency return  r_ fx   = change in the exchange rate, from the home investor&#x27;s point of view. return on a foreign investment as the home investor sees it. do not simply add the asset return and the currency move.\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">18</span><h3>Home-currency return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$(1 + R_{\\text{home}}) = (1 + R_{\\text{asset}})\\,(1 + R_{FX})$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$R_{FX}$ = change in the exchange rate, from the home investor's point of view.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Return on a foreign investment as the home investor sees it.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$(1.10)(0.88) - 1 = -3.2\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Do not simply add the asset return and the currency move.</p></div></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-global\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-19\" data-ff-ch=\"c3\" data-ff-text=\"net trading profit and costs  c  = commission on each side of the trade. what a trade really made once commissions are paid. trading costs can wipe out an apparent gain. net return on total outlay break-even price rise many trades advisory fee\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">19</span><h3>Net trading profit and costs</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Net profit} = N\\,(P_1 - P_0) - (C_{\\text{buy}} + C_{\\text{sell}})$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$C$ = commission on each side of the trade.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> What a trade really made once commissions are paid.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$1{,}000(0.30) - 60 = \\text{RM}240$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Trading costs can wipe out an apparent gain.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net return on total outlay</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{net}} = \\dfrac{\\text{net profit}}{N P_0 + C_{\\text{buy}}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Break-even price rise</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\Delta P = \\dfrac{C_{\\text{buy}} + C_{\\text{sell}}}{N}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Many trades</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$k\\,(C_{\\text{buy}} + C_{\\text{sell}})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Advisory fee</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Fee} = \\text{AUM} \\times f,\\qquad R_{\\text{net}} = R_{\\text{gross}} - f$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-protect\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-20\" data-ff-ch=\"c3\" data-ff-text=\"order execution rules  p  = market price,  l  = limit price,  s  = stop price. decide whether an order will execute, and at what price. a stop price is a trigger, not a guaranteed price: it becomes a market order when hit. stop triggers loss after a gap fill-or-kill\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">20</span><h3>Order execution rules</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Buy limit fills if } P \\le L,\\qquad \\text{Sell limit fills if } P \\ge L$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$P$ = market price, $L$ = limit price, $S$ = stop price.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Decide whether an order will execute, and at what price.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} \\text{Bought at } \\text{RM}20,\\ \\text{stop at } \\text{RM}18,\\ \\text{price gaps to } \\text{RM}14 \\\\ \\text{Loss} = 1{,}000\\,(20 - 14) = \\text{RM}6{,}000 \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A stop price is a trigger, not a guaranteed price: it becomes a market order when hit.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Stop triggers</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Sell stop: } P \\le S,\\qquad \\text{Buy stop: } P \\ge S$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Loss after a gap</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Loss} = N\\,(P_0 - P_{\\text{exec}})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Fill-or-kill</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Execute only if } Q_{\\text{available}} \\ge Q_{\\text{order}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-orders\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-21\" data-ff-ch=\"c3\" data-ff-text=\"relative performance in percentage points. a return means nothing without a matching benchmark. the benchmark must match the portfolio, or the comparison is unreliable.\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">21</span><h3>Relative performance</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Relative performance} = R_{\\text{portfolio}} - R_{\\text{benchmark}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">In percentage points.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A return means nothing without a matching benchmark.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$8\\% - 20\\% = -12\\ \\text{percentage points}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The benchmark must match the portfolio, or the comparison is unreliable.</p></div></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-indexes\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-22\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"long purchase cash purchase, no borrowing. the unlevered case, the benchmark for margin and short sales. long losses stop at zero; short losses do not.\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">22</span><h3>Long purchase</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Profit} = N\\,(P_1 - P_0) + \\text{dividends},\\qquad R = \\dfrac{P_1 - P_0}{P_0}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Cash purchase, no borrowing.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The unlevered case, the benchmark for margin and short sales.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{14 - 10}{10} = 40\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Long losses stop at zero; short losses do not.</p></div></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-short\">Practise in the lesson</button></article></div><div class=\"ff-tier\" data-ff-tier=\"3\"><h3 class=\"ff-tier-title\"><span>Tier 3</span> Good to know</h3><p class=\"ff-tier-note\">Occasional, mostly conceptual, or used inside the formulas above.</p><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-23\" data-ff-ch=\"c3\" data-ff-text=\"index construction  q  = number of shares outstanding. why different indexes report different &quot;market&quot; returns. a price-weighted index is dominated by the highest-priced stock; a value-weighted one by the largest company. average price value-weighted change equal-weighted change\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">23</span><h3>Index construction</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Price-weighted change} = \\dfrac{\\sum P_1}{\\sum P_0} - 1$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$Q$ = number of shares outstanding.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Why different indexes report different \"market\" returns.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{10 + 20 + 99}{10 + 20 + 90} - 1 = 7.5\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A price-weighted index is dominated by the highest-priced stock; a value-weighted one by the largest company.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Average price</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\bar{P} = \\dfrac{\\sum P}{n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Value-weighted change</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{\\sum P_1 Q}{\\sum P_0 Q} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Equal-weighted change</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum R_i$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-indexes\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-24\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"underwriting spread the bank buys at  p_  text paid    and sells to the public at  p_  text offer   . how the investment bank is paid, and who carries a shortfall. the underwriter bears the resale risk, not the issuer: the issuer already has its money. issuer proceeds bank profit or loss\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">24</span><h3>Underwriting spread</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Spread} = P_{\\text{offer}} - P_{\\text{paid to issuer}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">The bank buys at $P_{\\text{paid}}$ and sells to the public at $P_{\\text{offer}}$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How the investment bank is paid, and who carries a shortfall.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$20 - 19 = 1\\ \\text{per share}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The underwriter bears the resale risk, not the issuer: the issuer already has its money.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Issuer proceeds</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$N \\times P_{\\text{paid}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Bank profit or loss</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$N\\,(P_{\\text{resale}} - P_{\\text{paid}})$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-ipo\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-25\" data-ff-ch=\"c1 c3\" data-ff-text=\"bond prices, rates and yield a fixed coupon is worth less when new bonds pay more. direction questions: what happens to a bond price when rates move. the coupon is fixed; the price changes. coupon rate and yield are not the same thing. bond price as a present value current yield\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">25</span><h3>Bond prices, rates and yield</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c1\">Chapter 1</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$r \\uparrow \\;\\Rightarrow\\; P_{\\text{existing bond}} \\downarrow$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">A fixed coupon is worth less when new bonds pay more.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Direction questions: what happens to a bond price when rates move.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\text{old } 5\\% \\text{ bond, new bonds pay } 8\\% \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{price falls}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The coupon is fixed; the price changes. Coupon rate and yield are not the same thing.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Bond price as a present value</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\dfrac{C}{(1+y)^t} + \\dfrac{F}{(1+y)^n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Current yield</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Current yield} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual coupon}}{\\text{price}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l1-life\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-26\" data-ff-ch=\"c1\" data-ff-text=\"liquidity discount liquidity = fast conversion to cash with little loss of value. how much you give up to sell in a hurry. liquid does not mean safe, and market liquidity is not price stability.\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">26</span><h3>Liquidity discount</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c1\">Chapter 1</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Discount} = \\dfrac{\\text{fair value} - \\text{quick-sale price}}{\\text{fair value}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Liquidity = fast conversion to cash with little loss of value.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How much you give up to sell in a hurry.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{1{,}000{,}000 - 750{,}000}{1{,}000{,}000} = 25\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Liquid does not mean safe, and market liquidity is not price stability.</p></div></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l1-short\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-27\" data-ff-ch=\"c4 c5\" data-ff-text=\"risk and return identities diversification removes only the unsystematic part. which risk is rewarded in capm: only systematic risk. many holdings do not mean immunity from market risk. efficient frontier dominance return in one line\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">27</span><h3>Risk and return identities</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Total risk} = \\text{systematic (market)} + \\text{unsystematic (firm-specific)}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Diversification removes only the unsystematic part.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Which risk is rewarded in CAPM: only systematic risk.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\text{Forty bank stocks: still one bet on banking}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Many holdings do not mean immunity from market risk.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Efficient frontier dominance</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{same } \\sigma,\\ \\text{higher } E(R) \\quad\\text{or}\\quad \\text{same } E(R),\\ \\text{lower } \\sigma$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return in one line</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Return} = \\text{income} + \\text{change in value}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-beta\">Practise in the lesson</button></article></div></div>");
